B.oP=s([0,-128,64,-64,32,-96,96,-32,16,-112,80,-48,48,-80,112,-16,8,-120,72,-56,40,-88,104,-24,24,-104,88,-40,56,-72,120,-8,4,-124,68,-60,36,-92,100,-28,20,-108,84,-44,52,-76,116,-12,12,-116,76,-52,44,-84,108,-20,28,-100,92,-36,60,-68,124,-4,2,-126,66,-62,34,-94,98,-30,18,-110,82,-46,50,-78,114,-14,10,-118,74,-54,42,-86,106,-22,26,-102,90,-38,58,-70,122,-6,6,-122,70,-58,38,-90,102,-26,22,-106,86,-42,54,-74,118,-10,14,-114,78,-50,46,-82,110,-18,30,-98,94,-34,62,-66,126,-2,1,-127,65,-63,33,-95,97,-31,17,-111,81,-47,49,-79,113,-15,9,-119,73,-55,41,-87,105,-23,25,-103,89,-39,57,-71,121,-7,5,-123,69,-59,37,-91,101,-27,21,-107,85,-43,53,-75,117,-11,13,-115,77,-51,45,-83,109,-19,29,-99,93,-35,61,-67,125,-3,3,-125,67,-61,35,-93,99,-29,19,-109,83,-45,51,-77,115,-13,11,-117,75,-53,43,-85,107,-21,27,-101,91,-37,59,-69,123,-5,7,-121,71,-57,39,-89,103,-25,23,-105,87,-41,55,-73,119,-9,15,-113,79,-49,47,-81,111,-17,31,-97,95,-33,63,-65,127,-1],t.t)
B.bq4=s(["Still adjusting to life outside the womb \u2014 every system is activating.","Sleeps 16\u201320 hours per day in 2\u20134 hour cycles.","May lose up to 10% of birth weight this week \u2014 completely normal.","Cord stump still attached; keep it dry and clean.","Skin may be peeling, blotchy, or mildly jaundiced.","Reflexes present: rooting, sucking, grasping, Moro (startle).",u.F],t.s)
B.bW_=s(["Jaundice spreading below the belly button or to palms/soles","Not waking to feed after 4+ hours","Fewer than 6 wet diapers after day 4",u.l,"Difficulty breathing, blue-tinged lips or fingertips","Extreme limpness or unresponsiveness","Persistent crying that cannot be soothed"],t.s)
B.bsE=s(["Lochia (postpartum bleeding) is heavy and bright red \u2014 like a heavy period.",'Uterine contractions ("afterpains") as your uterus shrinks \u2014 more intense with breastfeeding.',"Perineal soreness if you had a vaginal birth; use ice packs and sitz baths.","C-section incision care: keep dry, watch for signs of infection.","Breast engorgement as milk comes in, typically days 2\u20135.","Night sweats are common \u2014 your body is shedding pregnancy fluids.","Haemorrhoids and constipation are common; stay hydrated, eat fibre."],t.s)
B.bLa=s(['"Baby blues" (weepiness, mood swings) are normal days 3\u20135 due to hormone shifts.',"These feelings typically resolve within 2 weeks.","If low mood persists beyond 2 weeks or feels severe \u2014 this may be postpartum depression. Seek support.","Bonding may not feel instant \u2014 this is normal and does not make you a bad mother.","Ask for help. You are not meant to do this alone."],t.s)
B.bKH=s(["Soaking more than 1 pad per hour for 2+ hours \u2014 call your provider","Fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F","Severe headache, especially with vision changes (signs of postpartum preeclampsia)","Signs of infection: redness, warmth, foul odour at incision or perineum","Chest pain or difficulty breathing","Thoughts of harming yourself or your baby \u2014 seek emergency help immediately"],t.s)
B.bSh=s(["Rest whenever possible \u2014 sleep when baby sleeps.","Accept all offers of help with meals and household tasks.","Stay hydrated: 8\u201310 glasses of water daily, especially if breastfeeding.","Eat nourishing foods \u2014 no need to diet right now.","Take your prenatal vitamins or postnatal supplement.","Gentle walks only if you feel up to it \u2014 no strenuous exercise yet."],t.s)
B.cGv=new A.lE(0,0,"Welcome to the World \ud83c\udf38",B.bq4,"Breastfeed 8\u201312 times per 24 hours \u2014 feed on demand, not by the clock. Watch for hunger cues: rooting, lip-smacking, fists to mouth. Formula: 1\u20132 oz every 2\u20133 hours. Weight loss is normal this week; regain expected by days 10\u201314. After day 4: 6+ wet diapers and 3\u20134 stools per day signal adequate intake. (AAP)","Newborns sleep 16\u201320 hours in fragmented cycles. Safe sleep (AAP): always on back, firm flat surface, no loose bedding, bumpers, or toys in sleep space. Room share (not bed share) for at least the first 6 months. Wake baby to feed if sleeping more than 3\u20134 hours in the first 2 weeks.",B.bW_,"Days 1\u20137: Your Body Begins to Heal",B.bsE,B.bLa,B.bKH,"\ud83d\udcc5 Newborn check at 3\u20135 days (AAP recommendation). Your own 1\u20132 week postpartum visit with OB/midwife.",B.bSh)
B.be0=s(["Should regain birth weight by days 10\u201314.","More alert periods appearing between sleeps.","Beginning to track faces and follow slow movements with eyes.","Startle (Moro) reflex very prominent.","Cord stump drying and darkening \u2014 will fall off at 1\u20133 weeks.","May show preference for familiar voices, especially mother's."],t.s)
B.bJT=s(["Not regaining birth weight by day 14","Yellow skin spreading or worsening after day 5","Fewer than 6 wet diapers per day","Cord stump showing redness, swelling, or foul smell","Fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F \u2014 emergency","Unusual high-pitched or inconsolable crying"],t.s)
B.bJR=s(["Lochia transitioning from red to pink or brownish.","Uterus continues to shrink \u2014 should be back to pre-pregnancy size by 6 weeks.","Perineal soreness improving; sitz baths remain helpful.","C-section incision closing \u2014 avoid lifting anything heavier than your baby.","Constipation common \u2014 drink plenty of fluids and eat fibre-rich foods.","Hair may begin to thin slightly \u2014 normal hormonal shift."],t.s)
B.bTj=s(["Hormone levels are still fluctuating significantly.","Oxytocin surges during breastfeeding promote bonding.","Sleep deprivation is at its most intense right now \u2014 rest is essential.","Feelings of isolation are common; reach out to other new parents.","If baby blues are still going strong past day 10, monitor closely."],t.s)
B.bRU=s(["Lochia suddenly becoming bright red or increasing in flow after 5+ days","Any fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F","Severe or worsening perineal pain","Persistent inability to sleep even when baby sleeps (anxiety/insomnia)","Feelings of detachment from your baby or from reality","Severe headache with visual disturbance \u2014 postpartum preeclampsia risk"],t.s)
B.bZN=s(["Prioritise rest \u2014 the dishes can wait.","Try to get one longer sleep stretch per night by handing off to a partner.","Shower daily if possible \u2014 small acts of self-care matter.","Stay connected: one supportive call or message per day helps.","Eat protein-rich foods to support healing.","Continue prenatal/postnatal vitamins; vitamin D if breastfeeding."],t.s)
B.cGB=new A.lE(1,1,"Regaining Strength \ud83d\udcaa",B.be0,"Breastfeeding becoming more established \u2014 nipple soreness should ease as latch improves. Cluster feeding (feeding every hour for several hours) is completely normal; it builds your milk supply. Watch for proper latch: baby's mouth wide open, lips flanged out, chin touching breast. Formula: 2\u20133 oz every 3\u20134 hours. If concerned about milk supply, see a lactation consultant. (WHO/AAP)","Still 16\u201318 hours of sleep per day. Continue waking baby to feed if sleeping more than 4 hours until birth weight is regained. Begin to notice your baby's sleep cues: yawning, eye-rubbing, looking away. Maintain safe sleep environment every time. (AAP)",B.bJT,"Week 2: Finding Your Footing",B.bJR,B.bTj,B.bRU,"\ud83d\udcc5 Your 1\u20132 week postpartum check with your OB or midwife. Newborn weight check with paediatrician.",B.bZN)
B.bkk=s(["Birth weight regained and growth continuing.","Cord stump likely fallen off by now \u2014 belly button healing.","Longer alert periods; beginning to respond to sounds.","May begin to calm at the sound of a familiar voice.","First social smiles still weeks away, but faces are fascinating.","Irregular sleep cycles continue \u2014 no predictable schedule yet."],t.s)
B.c28=s(["Redness or discharge from the belly button after cord falls off","Persistent yellow skin or eyes after day 14","Poor feeding or sustained weight loss","Breathing that seems rapid, laboured, or with grunting","Fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F"],t.s)
B.bUJ=s(["Lochia should be light pink, brown, or yellowish by now.","Energy levels beginning to return slightly.","Night sweats may persist for several weeks.","Incision or perineum continues healing; itching is normal.","First postpartum menstrual cycle may return if not breastfeeding.","Hair shedding (telogen effluvium) may begin \u2014 peaks around months 3\u20134."],t.s)
B.bef=s(["Baby blues should be resolving; if not, speak to your provider.","Anxiety is common in new parents \u2014 distinguish normal worry from excessive anxiety.","You may feel more confident with baby care by now.","Relationship dynamics shift after a new baby \u2014 communicate openly with your partner."],t.s)
B.bGT=s(["Lochia returning to bright red after lightening","Mood that feels overwhelming, hopeless, or disconnected beyond 2 weeks (postpartum depression \u2014 seek help)","Intrusive, disturbing thoughts that feel out of character (postpartum anxiety/OCD \u2014 treatable)","Any signs of wound infection","Urinary burning, frequency, or pain (UTI risk postpartum)"],t.s)
B.bGF=s(["Gentle walks outside are beneficial \u2014 fresh air lifts mood.","Try to eat at least one proper meal per day with your partner.","Communicate your needs \u2014 it's not weakness to ask.","If breastfeeding: continue calcium and vitamin D supplementation.","Begin gentle pelvic floor exercises (Kegels) if cleared by provider."],t.s)
B.cGD=new A.lE(2,2,"More Aware Every Day \ud83d\udc40",B.bkk,"Breastfeeding rhythm becoming more established. Supply regulates around this time \u2014 breasts may feel less full but milk is still plentiful. Feeding 8\u201312 times per 24 hours remains normal. If formula feeding: 2\u20133 oz every 3\u20134 hours; total 19\u201330 oz per day. Introduce bottle early if planning to mix-feed (but after breastfeeding is established). (AAP)","Baby still needs 15\u201318 hours of sleep. Differentiate day and night: bright, active days; dim, quiet nights. White noise can help soothe \u2014 keep volume below 50 dB. Safe sleep reminder: back to sleep, every sleep. (AAP)",B.c28,"Week 3: Early Recovery Continues",B.bUJ,B.bef,B.bGT,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 2\u20134 week paediatrician visit. Check that your own postpartum visit is scheduled.",B.bGF)
B.bJt=s(["Baby typically gains 150\u2013200 g (5\u20137 oz) per week at this stage.","More sustained eye contact and social engagement.","Cooing sounds may begin \u2014 early language development.","Hands are often fisted; may find fingers to suck.","Tummy time (supervised, when awake) important for neck and core strength.","Sleep remains fragmented with no consistent pattern."],t.s)
B.bUZ=s(["Poor weight gain (less than 150g/week consistently)","Excessive spitting up or projectile vomiting","Persistent crying for more than 3 hours per day (colic)","Rash or skin changes that spread or seem painful","Fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F"],t.s)
B.bTu=s(["Lochia should be minimal or stopped by now.","Physical energy noticeably returning for most women.","Perineum healed in most cases; C-section scar still tender.","Hormone levels stabilising slowly.","Libido typically low \u2014 normal; physical readiness for sex not until 6-week clearance.","Postpartum hair loss may be noticeable."],t.s)
B.bMT=s(['Adjustment to "new normal" of parenthood is ongoing.',"Many women feel more capable and confident by week 4.","Others feel overwhelmed \u2014 both are normal.","Stay connected socially, even briefly, each day.","Postpartum depression can emerge any time in the first year \u2014 remain aware."],t.s)
B.bUL=s(["Persistent sadness, hopelessness, or inability to function (postpartum depression)","Panic attacks or severe anxiety","Flashbacks or nightmares about birth (postpartum PTSD)","Any sudden heavy bleeding","Signs of mastitis: hard, red, painful breast area with fever"],t.s)
B.bR2=s(["You've made it through the first month \u2014 acknowledge that.","Consider a postpartum support group or online community.","Light exercise (walking) is beneficial if you feel ready.","Eat balanced meals \u2014 protein, healthy fats, colourful vegetables.","Continue to prioritise sleep over social obligations."],t.s)
B.cGG=new A.lE(3,3,"Growing Fast \ud83d\udcc8",B.bJt,"Breastfeeding well-established by now in most cases. Growth spurts common around 3 weeks \u2014 cluster feeding returns briefly. Formula: 3\u20134 oz every 3\u20134 hours; approximately 2.5 oz per pound of body weight per day. Begin daily supervised tummy time \u2014 2\u20133 minutes, several times per day. (AAP)","Some babies begin sleeping one slightly longer stretch at night (3\u20134 hours). Do not sleep train yet \u2014 too young. Swaddling can help comfort \u2014 ensure hips have room to move (no tight swaddle below hips). Safe sleep: firm, flat, no soft items. (AAP)",B.bUZ,"Week 4: Approaching Your First Month",B.bTu,B.bMT,B.bUL,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 1-month paediatrician check. Discuss your 6-week postpartum visit date with your provider.",B.bR2)
B.bHW=s(["The first true social smile typically appears around 6 weeks \u2014 a milestone!","More vocal: cooing, gurgling, and responding to your voice.","Follows moving objects with eyes across the midline.","Tummy time tolerance increasing; beginning to lift head briefly.","Growth spurts: baby may be fussier and hungrier than usual.","Sleep patterns still variable \u2014 no schedule expected yet."],t.s)
B.c0R=s(["No social smile by 8 weeks \u2014 mention to paediatrician","Not following objects with eyes","Poor feeding or very slow weight gain","Persistent excessive crying beyond 3 months (colic usually resolves)","Any fever over 38\xb0C / 100.4\xb0F"],t.s)
B.bVC=s(["Uterus fully returned to pre-pregnancy size by week 6.","Lochia has typically stopped.","C-section scar gradually flattening and fading.","Pelvic floor still weakened \u2014 Kegel exercises important.","Breast changes continue if breastfeeding.","Many women see first postpartum period by 6\u20138 weeks (if not breastfeeding).","Hair shedding continues \u2014 normal up to 6 months."],t.s)
B.bTd=s(["The 6-week mark is often seen as a milestone \u2014 but recovery continues well beyond.","Sleep deprivation is cumulative \u2014 be patient with yourself.","Relationship with partner may be strained \u2014 communication and patience are key.","Return to work planning may be causing anxiety \u2014 discuss concerns with your provider.","Screen for postpartum depression at your 6-week visit (Edinburgh Scale). (ACOG)"],t.s)
B.bvd=s(["Sadness or anxiety that interferes with daily life","Inability to bond with baby","Thoughts of self-harm or harming your baby \u2014 seek emergency help","Signs of mastitis: fever, red/painful breast area","Urinary incontinence or pain with urination","Pelvic pain or pelvic organ prolapse symptoms (pressure, bulge)"],t.s)
B.c1e=s(["At 6-week clearance: can resume sexual activity and most exercise if healing is complete.","Begin or intensify pelvic floor rehabilitation.","Consider seeing a pelvic floor physiotherapist \u2014 highly recommended.","Talk openly about contraception with your provider.","Take time for yourself, even 15 minutes, every day.","Rest is still essential \u2014 your body is still healing."],t.s)
B.cGC=new A.lE(4,5,"Social Smiles Arriving \ud83d\ude0a",B.bHW,"Continue breastfeeding on demand \u2014 supply is well-regulated by 6 weeks. If you've chosen to introduce a bottle, do so gently; some breastfed babies prefer specific teats. Formula: 4\u20135 oz every 3\u20134 hours by 6 weeks; total approximately 24\u201332 oz/day. No water or other fluids until 6 months. (WHO/AAP)","Some babies begin consolidating one longer nighttime stretch (4\u20135 hours). Introduce a simple bedtime routine: bath, feed, song, sleep. Continue safe sleep guidelines \u2014 firm, flat, back position always. (AAP) Overtiredness causes harder settling \u2014 watch for sleep cues.",B.c0R,"Weeks 4\u20136: Approaching Your Postpartum Check",B.bVC,B.bTd,B.bvd,"\ud83d\udcc5 Your 6-week postpartum check with OB/midwife (ACOG recommendation). Discuss contraception, pelvic floor, mood, and return to exercise. Baby's 2-month check-up and immunisations.",B.c1e)
B.c_4=s(["Social smiling in full bloom \u2014 responding to your smiles with their own.","Turning head toward sounds; beginning auditory localisation.","More purposeful arm and leg movements.","Tummy time: lifting head 45 degrees briefly.",'First "conversations" \u2014 cooing back and forth.',"Beginning to bat at objects within reach."],t.s)
B.bQ5=s(["Not making eye contact or responding to faces by 8 weeks","Very poor head control (floppy) \u2014 mention to paediatrician","Vomiting that is forceful or frequent (pyloric stenosis risk)","Skin rashes that look like tiny blisters or spreading rapidly","High-pitched or unusual cry"],t.s)
B.bRW=s(["Physical healing largely complete for most vaginal births.","C-section recovery ongoing \u2014 internal healing takes 3\u20136 months.","Pelvic floor still requires dedicated rehabilitation.","If breastfeeding, oestrogen remains low \u2014 vaginal dryness is common.","Energy levels improving but sleep debt still significant.","Hair shedding may be at its peak \u2014 temporary and will resolve."],t.s)
B.bRv=s(['Many women feel pressure to "bounce back" \u2014 resist this narrative.',"Postpartum depression can develop or worsen at this stage.","Returning to work (if soon) brings complex emotions \u2014 all valid.","Identity shift to motherhood is significant and takes time.","Seeking therapy or support is a sign of strength, not weakness."],t.s)
B.bZX=s(["Mood not improving or worsening after the 6-week check","Severe anxiety, panic, or intrusive thoughts","Mastitis symptoms: red, painful breast with fever \u2014 needs antibiotic treatment","Persistent pelvic pain or pressure","Any new or unusual physical symptoms"],t.s)
B.bQY=s(["Begin gentle exercise \u2014 postpartum yoga, swimming, or walking.","See a pelvic floor physiotherapist if you haven't already.","If returning to work soon, arrange feeding support or pumping schedule.","Continue to accept help and delegate household tasks.","Consider a postnatal check-in with a mental health professional."],t.s)
B.cGx=new A.lE(6,7,"Smiling & Discovering \ud83c\udf1f",B.c_4,"Breastfeeding supply is established and responsive to demand. If exclusively breastfeeding, no other foods or drinks needed. Formula: 5\u20136 oz per feed, roughly every 3\u20134 hours. Hunger cues remain the best guide for feeding frequency. Discuss vitamin D supplementation for breastfed babies (400 IU/day). (AAP)","Some babies now sleep 5\u20136 hours at night \u2014 celebrate this! Begin a consistent bedtime routine if you haven't already. Daytime naps: 3\u20134 naps, 30 minutes to 2 hours each. Total sleep: 14\u201317 hours per day. Safe sleep always applies. (AAP)",B.bQ5,"Weeks 6\u20138: After the 6-Week Check",B.bRW,B.bRv,B.bZX,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 2-month vaccines (important \u2014 do not delay). Follow up if you had a complicated birth or ongoing symptoms.",B.bQY)
B.c0Q=s(["First laughs emerging (some babies) \u2014 pure joy.","Holds head steadily when upright with support.","Discovers hands \u2014 watches them, brings them to mouth.","Pushes up on forearms during tummy time.","Responds to own name in familiar context.","Recognises parent's face and shows excitement.",'Increasing vocalisation and "chatting".'],t.s)
B.bQP=s(["Not smiling in response to your smile by 3 months","Not tracking objects smoothly with eyes","Floppy muscle tone or very stiff limbs","Not vocalising or responding to loud sounds","Persistent colic with no improvement"],t.s)
B.bY5=s(["Most women feel significantly more physically recovered.","Breastfeeding-related oestrogen suppression continues if nursing.","Pelvic floor healing ongoing \u2014 continue exercises.","Weight loss, if desired, can begin safely now with gentle nutrition changes.","C-section scar still healing internally \u2014 avoid high-impact exercise until 12 weeks.","Thyroid issues (postpartum thyroiditis) may emerge now \u2014 mention fatigue or mood changes to your doctor."],t.s)
B.bjP=s(["Adjustment to motherhood continues \u2014 there is no set timeline.","Sleep deprivation still affects mood and cognition \u2014 be compassionate with yourself.","Postpartum depression peaks in the first 3 months but can emerge any time in year 1. (ACOG)","Partner relationship: reconnect intentionally, even briefly each day.",'If you haven\'t found your "village" yet, actively seek connection.'],t.s)
B.bIZ=s(["Persistent low mood, anxiety, or emotional numbness","Not coping with daily tasks","Rage or anger that feels out of control","Physical symptoms not improving (pain, incontinence)","Thyroid symptoms: extreme fatigue, palpitations, weight changes"],t.s)
B.bRC=s(["Gradually build exercise \u2014 aim for 150 minutes of moderate activity per week. (WHO)","Join a new parent group for social connection.","Consider sleep consultants if exhaustion is severely impacting health.","Communicate your needs clearly to your partner or support network.","Celebrate milestones \u2014 yours and your baby's."],t.s)
B.cGE=new A.lE(8,11,"Laughing & Discovering Hands \ud83e\udd32",B.c0Q,"Continue breastfeeding or formula feeding on demand. Breastfed babies: no solid foods until 6 months (WHO recommendation). Formula: approximately 6 oz per feed, 5\u20136 feeds per day (~32 oz total). Growth spurts occur around 8 and 12 weeks \u2014 normal to feed more. Continue daily vitamin D for breastfed babies. (AAP)","Total sleep: 14\u201316 hours/day. Many babies now sleep 6\u20138 hours at night. Naps begin to consolidate: moving toward 3 per day. Watch for sleep regression around 8\u201310 weeks \u2014 temporary. Continue safe sleep practices. (AAP)",B.bQP,"Weeks 8\u201312: Finding Your Rhythm",B.bY5,B.bjP,B.bIZ,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 3-month paediatrician visit. Your own GP/provider check-in if you have ongoing concerns.",B.bRC)
B.bMi=s(["Beginning to roll from tummy to back (some babies).","Reaches for dangling objects purposefully.","Holds head up steadily during tummy time.","Laughs and squeals with delight.","Shows preference for familiar faces and may show stranger awareness.","Sleep becoming more organised with longer night stretches.","Brings objects to mouth to explore."],t.s)
B.bRN=s(["Not smiling or laughing by 4 months","Not reaching for objects",'Not making sounds or "talking"',"Poor head control by 4 months","Not rolling at all by 4 months (mention to paediatrician)"],t.s)
B.bXz=s(["Most physical postpartum symptoms resolved or resolving.","Hair loss (telogen effluvium) may peak and begin to slow.","Pelvic floor strengthening with consistent exercises.","Period may return if not breastfeeding; varies widely if breastfeeding.","Returning to full exercise capacity gradually."],t.s)
B.bXe=s(["Many women feel more settled and confident as parents by now.","Others continue to struggle \u2014 postpartum depression screening important.",'"Matrescence" \u2014 the identity transformation of becoming a mother \u2014 is a lifelong process.',"Social isolation remains a risk \u2014 prioritise connection.","Work-life balance concerns intensify for those returning to work."],t.s)
B.byx=s(["Ongoing depression, anxiety, or PTSD symptoms \u2014 seek professional support","Significant relationship conflict \u2014 couples counselling can help","Physical symptoms that haven't improved","Concerning changes in thyroid or other hormone-related symptoms"],t.s)
B.bNw=s(["Resume activities that brought you joy before baby.","Exercise regularly \u2014 it's one of the best mood stabilisers.","If returning to work, give yourself grace during the transition.","Maintain connection with your identity outside of motherhood.","Read, watch, or engage with things you enjoy."],t.s)
B.cGH=new A.lE(12,15,"Rolling & Reaching \ud83c\udfaf",B.bMi,"Continue breastfeeding or formula. No solids yet \u2014 digestive system not ready before 4\u20136 months. Formula: ~6\u20137 oz per feed, 4\u20135 feeds per day. Watch for signs of readiness for solids (around 6 months): sitting with support, losing tongue-thrust reflex. (AAP/WHO)","Many babies sleep 6\u20138 hours at night; some 10\u201312 hours. Daytime naps: typically 3, becoming more predictable. If baby wakes at night, give a chance to self-settle before intervening. Total sleep: 14\u201316 hours/day. Safe sleep still required. (AAP)",B.bRN,"Months 3\u20134: Emerging Confidence",B.bXz,B.bXe,B.byx,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 4-month vaccinations and check-up. Continue attending your own GP check-ups.",B.bNw)
B.bHa=s(["Rolling both ways: tummy to back and back to tummy.","Sitting with support; trunk control developing.",'Babbling with consonant sounds: "ba", "ma", "da".',"Recognises own name when called.","Grasps objects with both hands and transfers between hands.","Shows clear joy, frustration, and surprise.","May begin teething \u2014 drooling increases."],t.s)
B.aW8=s(["No babbling or vocalising","Not responding to own name","Not rolling in either direction by 5 months","Not sitting with support by 5 months","Hearing concerns: not startling at loud sounds"],t.s)
B.bPt=s(["Body largely recovered for most women.","Continued breastfeeding changes body composition; hunger remains increased.","Pelvic floor improving with consistent rehabilitation.","Libido may begin to return (varies widely).","Hair growth re-establishing after shedding phase."],t.s)
B.c_3=s(["Postpartum depression can still emerge \u2014 awareness remains important.","Many women feel more like themselves by now.","Anxiety around baby's development is common \u2014 trust your instincts.",'"Good enough" parenting is truly good enough \u2014 perfectionism is exhausting.'],t.s)
B.bVT=s(["Mood that feels persistently low or anxious","Continued inability to bond with baby","Relationship breakdown with partner","Physical symptoms: persistent pelvic pain, incontinence"],t.s)
B.bVw=s(["Plan a date night or solo time \u2014 even 1 hour away is restorative.","Continue exercise; strength training beneficial from this point.","Check in honestly with your mental health.","Invest in your relationship with your partner.","Begin researching solid food introduction for when baby is ready."],t.s)
B.cGy=new A.lE(16,19,"Rolling Both Ways & Babbling \ud83d\udde3\ufe0f",B.bHa,"Continue breastfeeding or formula as primary nutrition. If approaching 6 months, watch for solid-food readiness signs: sitting with support, head control, interest in food, loss of tongue-thrust reflex. Do not introduce solids before 4 months (AAP) and ideally not before 6 months (WHO). Formula: 6\u20138 oz per feed, 4\u20135 times per day.","4-month sleep regression is common and significant \u2014 babies' sleep architecture matures. This is temporary but exhausting. Stay consistent with routine. Total sleep: 12\u201315 hours/day. Daytime: 3 naps moving to 2. Nighttime: 8\u201310 hours with 1\u20132 wakings normal.",B.aW8,"Months 4\u20135: Settling In",B.bPt,B.c_3,B.bVT,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 4-month check-up and vaccines (if not done). 6-month well-baby visit approaching \u2014 schedule now.",B.bVw)
B.bC6=s(["Sitting briefly without support (by 6 months).","Reaches accurately and grasps objects well.","Passes objects from hand to hand.","Babbling increasing: longer strings of sounds.","Shows stranger anxiety beginning.","First teeth may emerge (lower front teeth usually first).","Shows clear preferences for toys, people, and activities."],t.s)
B.b7f=s(["Not sitting with any support by 6 months","Not reaching for objects","No babbling","Not bearing weight on legs when supported","Loss of skills previously achieved (always investigate)"],t.s)
B.bjT=s(["Six months postpartum \u2014 significant milestone.","Most physical recovery complete for uncomplicated vaginal birth.","C-section: internal healing largely complete, scar still softening.","Breastfeeding continues to suppress ovulation in many women.","Pelvic floor largely rehabilitated with consistent work."],t.s)
B.beo=s(["Identity as a mother becoming more integrated.","Many women feel more confident and capable.","Relationship with body changing \u2014 both physically and emotionally.","Continue to monitor for late-onset postpartum depression.","Grief over pre-baby life is real and valid \u2014 it coexists with love."],t.s)
B.bCb=s(["Onset of depressive or anxiety symptoms at 6 months (late-onset PPD)","Significant weight loss or disordered eating patterns","Pelvic floor symptoms not improving","Any concerns about your health \u2014 advocate for yourself"],t.s)
B.bSa=s(["Celebrate six months \u2014 you have done something extraordinary.","Resume regular exercise routine that you enjoy.","Invest in social connection \u2014 friendships that fell away may need nurturing.","Breastfeeding mothers: maintain calcium, vitamin D, omega-3 intake.","Plan something for yourself: a trip, a hobby, a goal."],t.s)
B.cGA=new A.lE(20,23,"Almost Ready for Food! \ud83e\udd44",B.bC6,"At 6 months: time to introduce solid foods! Start with smooth purees or soft finger foods (baby-led weaning). First foods: single-ingredient purees (sweet potato, pea, butternut squash, banana). Introduce common allergens early and one at a time (AAP recommends not delaying allergenic foods). Breastmilk or formula remains primary nutrition through 12 months. (AAP/WHO)","Total sleep: 12\u201315 hours/day. Two naps per day becoming the pattern. Nighttime: some babies sleep through, others still wake 1\u20132 times. Gentle sleep coaching approaches appropriate from 6 months if needed.",B.b7f,"Months 5\u20136: Six-Month Milestone",B.bjT,B.beo,B.bCb,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 6-month check-up and vaccines (important milestone visit). Your own annual check-up if overdue.",B.bSa)
B.bY1=s(["Sitting independently and confidently.","Crawling (7\u201310 months); some babies skip straight to walking.",'Pulls to stand holding furniture ("cruising").',"Pincer grasp developing \u2014 picks up small objects precisely.",'Says "mama" or "dada" with meaning.',"Plays peek-a-boo and claps hands.","Separation anxiety increases \u2014 a sign of healthy attachment.","Solid foods expanding: 2\u20133 meals per day, wide variety."],t.s)
B.byA=s(["Not sitting independently by 9 months","No pincer grasp developing by 9 months","Not babbling or pointing by 9 months","Loss of any previously acquired skill (always urgent \u2014 see paediatrician)","Not bearing weight on legs","No response to name by 9 months"],t.s)
B.bRZ=s(["Body largely returned to pre-pregnancy baseline for most women.","Period returns for breastfeeding mothers as nursing reduces in frequency.","Continued weight redistribution \u2014 body shape may be different, and that's okay.","Pelvic floor should be significantly stronger with consistent work.","Energy levels generally good unless sleep deprivation continues."],t.s)
B.bGc=s(["Postpartum depression can still present in months 6\u20139 \u2014 remain aware.","Celebrate how far you've come.","Maternal identity more settled, though always evolving.","Work-life balance ongoing negotiation.","Reconnection with partner relationship important."],t.s)
B.bI3=s(["Persistent mood symptoms at any point in year one","Extreme fatigue beyond normal parenting tiredness","Any physical symptoms that feel off \u2014 trust your instincts","Increased alcohol or substance use as coping"],t.s)
B.bLE=s(["Maintain regular exercise \u2014 one of the best investments in your health.","Prioritise sleep whenever possible.","Nurture your relationship with your partner intentionally.","Pursue something just for you: learning, creating, exploring.","Community matters \u2014 stay connected to people who fill your cup."],t.s)
B.cGz=new A.lE(24,35,"Crawling & Clapping \ud83d\udc4f",B.bY1,"Three meals of solid foods per day by 9\u201310 months. Introduce wide variety of flavours and textures \u2014 reduces picky eating later. Breastmilk or formula still primary, reducing as solid intake increases. Water in a sippy cup introduced at meals from 6 months. Avoid honey before 12 months (botulism risk). No added salt or sugar. Common allergens (peanut, egg, milk, wheat) introduced and maintained. (AAP)","Total sleep: 12\u201314 hours/day. Two naps (morning and afternoon) until around 15\u201318 months. Most babies sleep through the night by 9 months; many still wake. Sleep regressions at 8 and 9 months are common. Safe sleep: firm flat surface, no pillows/blankets until 12 months. (AAP)",B.byA,"Months 6\u20139: Your New Normal",B.bRZ,B.bGc,B.bI3,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 9-month developmental check. Your annual well-woman exam.",B.bLE)
B.bw2=s(["Most babies take first steps between 9\u201312 months.",'First meaningful words emerging ("mama", "dada", "ball").',"Understands many more words than they can say.","Points to show interest \u2014 crucial communication milestone.","Plays simple imitation games.","Self-feeding with fingers; beginning to use spoon.","Shows clear emotions: joy, frustration, affection.","Waves goodbye; responds to simple instructions."],t.s)
B.bJu=s(["No words at all by 12 months","Not pointing or waving by 12 months","Not walking with support by 12 months","No response to name","Loss of any previously acquired skills (developmental concern \u2014 see paediatrician)","No back-and-forth communication by 12 months"],t.s)
B.bS9=s(["Full physical recovery complete for most women.","If breastfeeding, body supporting both you and your growing baby.","Period cycle typically regular by now.","Pelvic floor: continue maintenance exercises long-term.","Body may look and feel different \u2014 this is your body having done something incredible."],t.s)
B.bN3=s(["Approaching your baby's first birthday brings complex emotions for many mothers.","Reflection on the year \u2014 give yourself immense credit.","Postpartum depression: still possible to emerge. Any time, first year.","Identity as a mother now deeply integrated.","Goals and ambitions outside motherhood deserve space."],t.s)
B.bPo=s(["Late-onset postpartum depression \u2014 still possible in months 9\u201312","Relationship in significant distress","Physical symptoms that have lingered \u2014 advocate for a thorough evaluation","Anxiety about child's development that feels overwhelming"],t.s)
B.c_A=s(["Plan something to mark your baby's first birthday AND your own survival of the first year.","Reflect on how much you've grown.","Invest in your health long-term: regular exercise, good nutrition, sleep.","Your mental health matters \u2014 seek support proactively, not only in crisis.","Look forward: what do you want this next year to look like?"],t.s)
B.cGw=new A.lE(36,51,"Walking & First Words \ud83d\udc63",B.bw2,"By 12 months: transition to whole cow's milk (approximately 16\u201324 oz/day). (AAP) Three meals plus 2 snacks per day. Diet should include protein, healthy fats, iron-rich foods, fruits, and vegetables. If breastfeeding: WHO recommends continuing as long as mutually desired (beyond 1 year). Continue avoiding honey, choking hazards (whole grapes, nuts), and added sugar.","Total sleep: 11\u201314 hours/day. Two naps until 15\u201318 months. Many toddlers still wake at night \u2014 this is within normal range. Sleep regressions common around 12 months (developmental leap). Remove bottle from sleep routine at 12 months to protect teeth.",B.bJu,"Months 9\u201312: Almost a Year",B.bS9,B.bN3,B.bPo,"\ud83d\udcc5 Baby's 12-month well-child visit and immunisations. Discuss transition to cow's milk and diet changes. Your annual well-woman exam.",B.c_A)
B.c0X=s(["Walking independently (most between 9\u201315 months \u2014 wide normal range).","Vocabulary growing: 1\u20133 words at 12 months, 10\u201350 by 18 months.","Pretend play beginning around 12\u201318 months.","Self-feeding skills improving.","Strong social and emotional development \u2014 attachment to caregivers deep.","Rapid cognitive development: object permanence, cause-and-effect.","Developmental checkpoints continue at 15, 18, 24 months and beyond."],t.s)
B.bG3=s(["Not walking by 18 months (mention to paediatrician)","Fewer than 6 words by 18 months","Loss of any speech or skills at any age \u2014 seek prompt assessment","Not engaging in back-and-forth play by 18 months","Developmental concerns \u2014 always raise with your paediatrician"],t.s)
B.bPJ=s(["Your body is yours again \u2014 largely recovered from pregnancy and birth.","Long-term pelvic floor health: continue exercises and see a physio if needed.","Hormones stabilised whether breastfeeding or not.","Any lingering physical issues deserve proper assessment \u2014 you've earned good care."],t.s)
B.bZc=s(["A year of extraordinary love, sacrifice, growth, and strength.","Matrescence \u2014 the transformation of becoming a mother \u2014 continues for life.","Your identity as a woman and as a mother both deserve nourishment.","If you struggled this year, you're not alone. If you thrived, celebrate.","You know your child and yourself better than anyone."],t.s)
B.bQr=s(["Persistent or newly emerging mental health concerns \u2014 always worth addressing","Physical symptoms that have gone unaddressed \u2014 prioritise your healthcare","Relationship concerns \u2014 invest in your relationship before it deteriorates further"],t.s)
B.bUK=s(["You have survived and thrived through one of life's most transformative experiences.","Prioritise your health as a lifelong investment \u2014 for you and for your family.","Stay connected to your support network.","Pursue your own goals, dreams, and joys.","Keep an eye on your mental health \u2014 maternal mental health matters always.","You are more than a mother. You are a whole person. Honour that."],t.s)
B.cGF=new A.lE(52,999,"One Year Old & Beyond! \ud83c\udf82",B.c0X,"Whole cow's milk: 16\u201324 oz/day (AAP). Family foods with variety; no special toddler foods needed. Avoid added sugars, excessive salt, and choking hazards. Iron-rich foods important \u2014 toddlers at risk for iron deficiency. If breastfeeding continues: wonderful \u2014 WHO supports this beyond 1 year. Picky eating common at 18\u201324 months \u2014 keep offering, don't force.","Total sleep: 11\u201314 hours. Transition to one nap around 15\u201318 months. Bedtime routine is essential for toddler sleep. Screen time before bed disrupts sleep \u2014 limit to 1 hour of quality content/day and none before bed. (AAP)",B.bG3,"One Year: You Did It. \ud83d\udc9c",B.bPJ,B.bZc,B.bQr,"\ud83d\udcc5 Annual well-woman exam (ACOG recommends yearly). Baby's 15 and 18-month check-ups. Continue regular paediatric visits per schedule.",B.bUK)
B.a4j=s([B.cGv,B.cGB,B.cGD,B.cGG,B.cGC,B.cGx,B.cGE,B.cGH,B.cGy,B.cGA,B.cGz,B.cGw,B.cGF],A.al("M<lE>"))
B.Hl=new A.b2l(2,"medium")
B.rJ=new A.F3(0,"denied")
B.z8=new A.F3(1,"deniedForever")
B.Hm=new A.F3(2,"whileInUse")
B.c2k=new A.F3(3,"always")
B.c2l=new A.LQ(B.Hl,100,null)
B.aU=new A.W6(0,"ignored")
B.f6=new A.O(4294967304)
B.rL=new A.O(4294967323)
B.f7=new A.O(4294967423)
B.Hp=new A.O(4294967558)
B.rQ=new A.O(8589934848)
B.ze=new A.O(8589934849)
B.kQ=new A.O(8589934850)
B.my=new A.O(8589934851)
