B.cyz={"Night Sweats":0,"Perineal Pain":1,"Baby Blues / Mood":2,"Breast Soreness":3,Fatigue:4}
B.dd7=new A.h9(7,12,20,5,8,14,7,"Night sweats that are persistent beyond the early postpartum window, worsening, or paired with fever, chills, or feeling unwell need provider review to rule out infection or endocrine causes.","Night sweats are a common postpartum recovery pattern in the first days to weeks after birth as estrogen falls and extra fluid is shed.","Occasional night sweats can be normal postpartum. Focus on hydration, breathable clothing, and monitor for fever or other illness symptoms.")
B.dda=new A.h9(7,10,16,5,8,12,7,"Perineal pain that is severe, worsening, foul-smelling, or not improving over time needs provider review to rule out infection, hematoma, or delayed healing.","Perineal pain can be common early postpartum, especially after tearing or stitches, but the overall trend should be gradual improvement.","Mild perineal soreness is common in early recovery. Ice packs, sitz baths, rest, and pain relief approved by your provider can help.")
B.dd8=new A.h9(7,10,16,4,7,11,10,"Mood symptoms that are intense, worsening, involve hopelessness or intrusive thoughts, or persist beyond the first 2 weeks postpartum need screening for postpartum depression or anxiety.","Mood shifts are common in the first days after birth, but patterns that continue beyond the baby-blues window deserve follow-up and support.","Brief tearfulness, overwhelm, or mood swings can be part of the baby blues in the first 1 to 2 weeks postpartum.")
B.dd4=new A.h9(7,12,18,5,8,12,7,"Breast pain that is worsening or paired with fever, redness, a hot area, or flu-like symptoms needs prompt review for mastitis or significant engorgement.","Breast soreness is common when milk comes in and latch is being established, but it should become more manageable with support.","Mild breast soreness is common postpartum, especially during feeding transitions or engorgement.")
B.dd3=new A.h9(7,12,22,5,8,15,7,"Extreme postpartum fatigue that is worsening or associated with dizziness, breathlessness, or heavy bleeding needs provider review.","Frequent fatigue is common in postpartum recovery, but support, rest, and monitoring for anemia matter.","Some fatigue is expected while recovering and caring for a newborn.")
